## Proof

Recall that a morphism of schemes $f: Z \to W$ is **quasi-compact** if for every quasi-compact open subset $U \subseteq W$, the preimage $f^{-1}(U)$ is a quasi-compact topological space (equivalently, a quasi-compact scheme).

We claim that the inclusion $i: Y \to X$ of a closed subscheme is quasi-compact.

**Setup.** Let $U \subseteq X$ be a quasi-compact open subset. We must show that $i^{-1}(U)$ is quasi-compact.

**Identifying the preimage.** The morphism $i: Y \to X$ is a closed immersion. The scheme-theoretic preimage $i^{-1}(U)$ is the fiber product $Y \times_X U$, which is an open subscheme of $Y$. Its underlying topological space is homeomorphic to $U \cap Y$, where $Y$ is viewed as a closed subset of the underlying topological space of $X$ (with the subspace topology).

**Key topological fact.** Since $Y$ is a closed subscheme, $Y$ is a **closed** subset of the topological space $|X|$. Therefore $U \cap Y$ is a closed subset of $U$ (in the subspace topology on $U$).

**Closed subsets of quasi-compact spaces are quasi-compact.** This is a standard topological result: if $V$ is a quasi-compact topological space and $F \subseteq V$ is closed, then $F$ is quasi-compact. Indeed, given any open cover $\{W_\alpha\}$ of $F$ (by sets open in $F$), write $W_\alpha = F \cap O_\alpha$ for opens $O_\alpha$ of $V$. Then $\{O_\alpha\} \cup \{V \setminus F\}$ is an open cover of $V$. By quasi-compactness of $V$, finitely many suffice; the corresponding $W_\alpha$ (discarding $V \setminus F$) cover $F$.

**Conclusion.** Since $U$ is quasi-compact and $U \cap Y$ is a closed subset of $U$, the space $U \cap Y$ is quasi-compact. Hence $i^{-1}(U)$ is quasi-compact.

Since $U$ was an arbitrary quasi-compact open subset of $X$, the morphism $i: Y \to X$ is quasi-compact. $\blacksquare$

